-- Care Chart week-by-week content, Third trimester (weeks 27-39).
-- Same fuller card structure as First/Second trimester: theme, mantra,
-- priority, journey, what-you-may-notice, Move (5/15/30-min tiers),
-- Nourish, Hydration goal, Reset (5-mood-mapped), Care for yourself,
-- Your corner, Support moment, Celebrate this week, For your care team.
--
-- Content: Claude drafted a base version first (weeks 27-39, grounded
-- against ACOG/Count the Kicks/other sources for kick-count guidance,
-- GBS screening timing, and Braxton Hicks vs true labour differentiation),
-- Roop ran it past other AI apps, and returned "Third Trimester -- Final
-- Move and Reset Edition." Diffed programmatically against Claude's base
-- draft: Move/Reset card structure, most Care-for-yourself/Your corner/
-- Support moment/Celebrate/For your care team content, and 3 of 13 weeks
-- (27, 29, 33) are unchanged. The reviewed edition's real, substantive
-- changes over the base draft -- independently verified before locking:
--   * Week 28 -- generalised the kick-count method from Claude's specific
--     "10 movements" framing to "follow your care team's local guidance,"
--     avoiding asserting one specific counting protocol as universal.
--   * Week 32 -- corrected a real safety gap in Claude's draft: the base
--     draft suggested a home-hydration test to settle worry about
--     tightening; the reviewed edition correctly redirects to prompt
--     clinical advice for regular/frequent tightenings before 37 weeks
--     rather than a home test.
--   * Weeks 34/36/37/39 -- added precise ACOG gestational-age terminology
--     (early term 37 0/7-38 6/7 weeks, full term from 39 0/7 weeks, 34-36
--     weeks still preterm) -- verified against ACOG Committee Opinion 579.
--   * Week 35 -- added the precise ACOG GBS screening window (36 0/7-37
--     6/7 weeks, verified against ACOG) and softened "prevents almost all
--     newborn complications" to the more medically accurate "reduces, not
--     eliminates, the risk."
--   * Week 38 -- added a caution against unproven self-induction methods
--     and clearer waters-breaking guidance.
--   * Week 30 -- added specific urgent-breathlessness red flags (sudden,
--     severe, at rest, or with chest pain/fainting/palpitations/one-sided
--     leg swelling) consistent with this project's already-verified
--     maternal warning-signs list.
-- Every other card is Roop's own reviewed content, transcribed verbatim.
-- Parsed programmatically from the docx (not hand-transcribed) and
-- round-trip validated through the same JSON-escaping path the SQL
-- generator uses -- zero errors across all 26 move/reset jsonb blocks.

insert into care_chart_week_content
  (week_number, trimester, theme_title, mantra, priority, journey, what_you_may_notice, move, nourish, hydration_goal, reset, care_for_yourself, your_corner, support_moment, celebrate_this_week, for_your_care_team)
values
  (27, 'third', 'Welcome to the final stretch', 'You''re not just further along — you''re in a different phase now.', 'Notice how the third trimester feels different in your body, and let your pace shift with it.', 'You''ve crossed into the third trimester. Your baby is gaining fat and practising breathing motions; you may feel steadier some days and more tired on others as your body carries more weight.', ARRAY['- A new kind of tiredness, different from early pregnancy fatigue','A more noticeable, harder-feeling bump','Backache or pelvic heaviness by the end of the day','A mix of excitement and "there''s still so far to go"']::text[], '{"focus": "Shift from \"how much can I do\" to \"how does this feel today\" — the right question for the rest of pregnancy.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Sit supported, do 8 ankle circles per side and take six slow breaths.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes at an easy pace, then do 5 minutes of shoulder rolls and standing pelvic tilts.", "feeling_good": "Walk or swim for 20 minutes, then one round of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 8 supported side leg lifts per side."}, "mood_adjustment": "Tired: five minutes is a full session. Heavy or achy: choose water-based movement if available. Energised: enjoy it, but stop before you''re depleted.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Your baby''s fat stores are building — small, protein-rich meals every few hours (dal, eggs, curd, paneer, nuts) can help more than three large ones now that space is tighter.', 'Keep a bottle within reach at all times — you''ll need to remember more often now that sitting for long stretches is less comfortable.', '{"heavy_day": "If \"still so far to go\" feels heavy, shrink the day to just today. You don''t have to feel ready for the whole trimester at once.", "a_little_low": "Name what''s actually tired — body, mind, or both. They sometimes need different things.", "okay": "Take two minutes to notice one thing that''s easier now than in week one, and one thing that''s harder. Both are allowed to be true.", "good": "Use the steadier mood to start a loose mental list of what \"the final stretch\" means to you — not a task list, just noticing.", "really_good": "Let this feel good without bracing for it to change. Write down one thing about today you''d want to remember."}'::jsonb, 'Skin may feel tighter as your bump grows — a rich, fragrance-free moisturiser after a shower can ease itchiness. Continue broad-spectrum sunscreen if you''re outdoors.', 'Notice one thing your body has done well this week — however small.', 'Tell someone close to you what "final stretch" support would look like — more rest, more help, or just company.', 'You''ve reached the third trimester. Whatever this week looked like, you carried it.', 'Ask about your appointment schedule from here — visits usually become more frequent through the third trimester.'),
  (28, 'third', 'Getting to know the pattern', 'You don''t need a perfect count — just a familiar one.', 'Start noticing your baby''s usual movement pattern, so you''d recognise a real change.', 'Many people begin paying closer attention to fetal movement from around now. Your baby has active and quiet periods. Follow your care team''s local guidance for movement awareness or counting, and call promptly if movement is clearly reduced or different for your baby.', ARRAY['- Stronger, more distinct kicks and rolls','Movement that clusters at certain times of day','Mild swelling in your feet or ankles by evening','Heartburn or shortness of breath as your uterus rises']::text[], '{"focus": "Use gentle movement to notice your body, not to test it.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Sit supported, complete 10 ankle pumps per side, and take five slow breaths. Movement exercise is for your comfort — not a way to test your baby.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes, then 5 minutes of seated ankle pumps and gentle side stretches.", "feeling_good": "Walk or swim for 20 minutes, then one round of 8 wall pelvic tilts, 8 supported side leg lifts per side and 8 heel raises."}, "mood_adjustment": "Breathless: choose water-based movement or slower pace. Swollen: elevate your feet before and after. Energised: keep it moderate rather than pushing duration.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Iron needs rise now — pair lentils, greens, eggs or meat with a vitamin-C food (citrus, tomato, capsicum) to help your body use it.', 'If heartburn is bothering you, try smaller, more frequent sips rather than large drinks with meals.', '{"heavy_day": "If movement awareness feels like one more worry, use the method your care team has given you. If movement is clearly reduced or different, contact them promptly rather than trying to reassure yourself at home.", "a_little_low": "If today''s pattern felt quieter than usual, it''s always fair to check with your clinician rather than sit with the worry.", "okay": "Pick a time of day — after a meal or when you sit down to rest — that becomes your own quiet check-in moment.", "good": "Use the steadier mood to notice what your baby''s \"usual\" is starting to feel like. You''re building real, useful knowledge.", "really_good": "Enjoy a strong, active stretch of movement without overanalysing it. Let it just be a good sign."}'::jsonb, 'If your feet are swelling, loosen or size up footwear rather than pushing through discomfort. This is common, not a failure of care.', 'Describe today''s movement in your own words — a wiggle, a somersault, a stretch — and save it.', 'Invite someone to feel a kick with you, if and when it happens to line up.', 'You''re learning your baby''s language before you''ve even met.', 'Ask what your clinic recommends for tracking movement, and what they''d want you to call about.'),
  (29, 'third', 'Carrying more, gently', 'Support isn''t giving up — it''s what lets you keep going.', 'Reduce strain on your back and pelvis as your weight distribution keeps shifting.', 'Your baby is putting on weight quickly now, and your centre of gravity keeps moving forward. Backache, pelvic pressure and slower movement are common as your body adjusts to carrying more.', ARRAY['- Lower back or pelvic ache, especially by evening','A waddling or careful walk','Trouble finding a comfortable sleep position','Round-ligament pulling with sudden movement']::text[], '{"focus": "Support your back and pelvis with slow, controlled movement.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Do 8 standing pelvic tilts with hands on a wall, then two minutes of slow breathing.", "steady": "Walk for 8 minutes, then 5 minutes of cat-cow on hands and knees (if comfortable) or standing pelvic tilts and side stretches.", "feeling_good": "Walk or swim for 15 minutes, then one round of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 8 standing pelvic tilts."}, "mood_adjustment": "Achy: choose water-based movement if you can — it takes weight off your joints. Frustrated by the slower pace: match effort to the day, not to a memory of how you used to move. Good: enjoy it without overdoing distance.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Calcium needs stay high — milk, curd, paneer, calcium-set tofu or other fortified options support your bones as your baby draws on your stores.', 'Regular fluids can ease constipation, which often makes backache feel worse. Pair water with fibre-rich meals.', '{"heavy_day": "Lie on your side with a pillow between your knees. You don''t need to solve the ache today — just be more comfortable for the next ten minutes.", "a_little_low": "Say: \"Slower is not the same as behind.\" Let one task wait until someone can help.", "okay": "Do a two-minute posture check: feet supported, shoulders soft, weight even. Comfort counts as productive.", "good": "Use the steadier window to set up one thing that''ll make tomorrow easier — an extra pillow, a lower shelf, a chair with support.", "really_good": "Notice a moment today when your body felt strong, not just heavy. Let that be the headline."}'::jsonb, 'A maternity support belt, a firmer mattress topper, or simply more pillows can meaningfully ease pelvic and back discomfort — worth experimenting with what helps you specifically.', 'Take a photo of a small comfort upgrade you made this week — a pillow fort, new shoes, anything.', 'Ask for help with anything that involves bending, lifting or reaching — it adds up more than it looks like it should.', 'You kept moving, even when it took more effort.', 'Mention pain that''s severe, one-sided, or comes with fever, bleeding or urinary symptoms — otherwise, ordinary aches are worth a mention, not a worry.'),
  (30, 'third', 'Making room to breathe', 'Make room for slower breaths — and take new or severe breathlessness seriously.', 'Adjust your pace as your lungs share space with a growing uterus.', 'As the uterus rises, ordinary activity may feel more breathless. Pregnancy can also affect the heart and lungs, so breathlessness that is sudden, severe, present at rest or accompanied by chest pain, fainting, palpitations or one-sided leg swelling needs urgent assessment.', ARRAY['- Breathlessness with ordinary activity like stairs','Rib discomfort, especially on one side','Heartburn or a feeling of fullness after small meals','Vivid dreams or lighter sleep']::text[], '{"focus": "Work with your breath rather than against it.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Sit tall, and practise slow belly breathing for five minutes — in through the nose, longer exhale out.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes at a pace where you can still talk, then 5 minutes of seated side stretches to open your ribcage.", "feeling_good": "Swim or walk for 20 minutes at conversational effort, then 10 minutes of gentle mobility: shoulder rolls, side stretches and standing pelvic tilts."}, "mood_adjustment": "Breathless: choose sitting or water-based movement over anything upright and sustained. Anxious about the sensation: slow down and notice whether it settles with rest. Do not assume breathlessness is only mechanical; contact your clinician for a new, worsening or worrying change. Good: keep effort easy regardless of how capable you feel.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness that comes on suddenly or feels severe, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Smaller, more frequent meals leave more room for your lungs and stomach, which are both sharing tighter space now.', 'Sip steadily through the day rather than drinking a large amount at once, which can worsen the fullness feeling.', '{"heavy_day": "Sit upright, shoulders back, and take slow breaths low into your belly rather than shallow ones into your chest. Give it a full two minutes.", "a_little_low": "If feeling breathless is scaring you, name that directly to your clinician rather than carrying the worry alone.", "okay": "Notice where you''re holding tension — often shoulders or jaw — and let it soften for one breath cycle.", "good": "Use the steadier moment to find a sleep position with pillows that eases both your ribs and your back.", "really_good": "Take five minutes of unhurried fresh air, noticing your breath without trying to change it."}'::jsonb, 'Loose, breathable clothing around your ribs and waist can meaningfully ease the tight, breathless feeling.', 'Write one sentence about how it feels to share your body so fully with someone else right now.', 'Ask someone to take over anything involving stairs or sustained standing this week.', 'Every breath is doing double duty right now. That''s remarkable, even on hard days.', 'Mention breathlessness that comes on suddenly, feels severe, or comes with chest pain — that''s always worth an urgent call, not a wait-and-see.'),
  (31, 'third', 'Rest is part of the plan', 'Swelling is common. Sudden or severe swelling is not the same thing.', 'Protect your sleep and manage ordinary swelling without ignoring the signs that need attention.', 'Your baby''s brain, lungs and other organs continue maturing quickly. Ordinary fluid retention often shows up in feet and ankles by evening; your own sleep may be more broken as comfortable positions become harder to find.', ARRAY['- Ankle or foot swelling that eases overnight','Broken or lighter sleep','Increased Braxton Hicks tightening','Restless legs or leg cramps, especially at night']::text[], '{"focus": "Use gentle movement to support circulation and ease swelling.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Sit with feet elevated and do 10 ankle circles per side and 10 toe points.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes, then 5 minutes of calf stretches and seated leg elevation.", "feeling_good": "Swim or walk for 20 minutes, then one round of 10 heel raises, 8 wall pelvic tilts and 8 supported side leg lifts per side."}, "mood_adjustment": "Swollen or achy: prioritise time with feet up over more movement. Exhausted from broken sleep: five minutes is enough. Restless: a rhythmic walk can help more than stillness.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'A balanced evening snack can help if hunger interrupts sleep. Persistent or painful leg cramps deserve discussion with your clinician; do not start magnesium supplements without personalised advice.', 'Keep fluids steady through the day unless your clinician has given a different plan. Do not restrict fluids simply to reduce ordinary swelling or night-time bathroom trips.', '{"heavy_day": "If broken sleep is wearing you down, let today have a lower bar. Rest whenever you get the chance, even in short stretches.", "a_little_low": "Say: \"Uncomfortable is not the same as unsafe.\" Most of what you''re feeling this week is common, even when it''s unpleasant.", "okay": "Take a two-minute body scan lying on your side, noticing where you''re carrying tension and letting it go one spot at a time.", "good": "Use the steadier mood to set up your sleep space for tonight — extra pillows, cooler room, whatever''s helped before.", "really_good": "If you get a good stretch of rest, let yourself enjoy it without guilt about \"not doing enough\" otherwise."}'::jsonb, 'Elevate your feet above heart level for 15-20 minutes when you can, and avoid standing still for long stretches.', 'Note one small comfort that helped you rest better this week, so you remember it next time.', 'Ask someone to take an evening task off your list so you can put your feet up earlier.', 'Rest is not a pause from preparing for birth — it is preparing for birth.', 'Report sudden or one-sided swelling, swelling with a headache or vision changes, or swelling above the ankles that appears quickly — these are different from ordinary end-of-day puffiness and deserve a same-day call.'),
  (32, 'third', 'Practice contractions, real preparation', 'A tightening belly can be your body rehearsing, not starting.', 'Get familiar with Braxton Hicks tightening so you can tell it apart from real labour later.', 'Braxton Hicks contractions — irregular, usually painless tightening — often become more noticeable now, as your uterus practises for labour. Your baby is continuing to gain weight steadily.', ARRAY['- Tightening across your belly that comes and goes irregularly','Tightening that eases with rest, position change or a warm drink','More pelvic pressure with activity','Continued swelling or breathlessness']::text[], '{"focus": "Notice how your body responds to movement without fearing every tightening.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Sit or lie on your side, and take five slow breaths while noticing whether any tightening eases as you rest.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes, then 5 minutes of gentle pelvic tilts and side stretches.", "feeling_good": "Swim or walk for 20 minutes, then one round of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 8 standing pelvic tilts."}, "mood_adjustment": "Worried about tightening: rest and see if it passes — that itself is useful information. Uncomfortable: choose the shorter option and prioritise rest. Good: enjoy movement without needing to \"test\" your body.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, contractions that become regular and don''t ease with rest, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Staying well fed and hydrated can genuinely reduce Braxton Hicks frequency — an empty stomach or dehydration are common triggers.', 'Maintain regular fluids. Before 37 weeks, regular or frequent tightenings, pelvic pressure, low backache, fluid leakage or a change in discharge need prompt clinical advice rather than a home hydration test.', '{"heavy_day": "If tightenings become regular, frequent, painful, do not ease, or come with pelvic pressure, backache, discharge change or fluid leakage before 37 weeks, contact your maternity team promptly. Rest while arranging advice.", "a_little_low": "It''s normal to feel unsettled by a new sensation. Naming it to your clinician is always reasonable, never an overreaction.", "okay": "Notice today''s tightening pattern without judging it — is it irregular, does it ease with rest? Just observe.", "good": "Use the steadier mood to learn the difference: true labour contractions get closer together, stronger, and don''t ease with rest or position change. Practice contractions usually do.", "really_good": "Let curiosity, not fear, be your relationship with your body''s rehearsals this week."}'::jsonb, 'A warm (not hot) shower or a change of position can often ease Braxton Hicks discomfort.', 'Write down what your Braxton Hicks feel like in your own words — it''ll help you notice if real labour feels different later.', 'Practice timing a tightening together, just so it feels familiar rather than alarming when it matters later.', 'Your body is quietly rehearsing for one of the biggest things it will ever do.', 'Ask your clinician directly what they want you to do if you notice regular, closer-together contractions before you''d expect labour — the answer differs depending on your history and weeks.'),
  (33, 'third', 'Your preferences belong in this', 'You''re allowed to have preferences, and to hold them loosely.', 'Start thinking through what matters to you for labour and birth — as a starting conversation, not a fixed script.', 'Your baby''s bones are hardening (except the skull, which stays flexible for birth) and their immune system is strengthening through antibodies you''re passing along. This is a common stage to start birth-preference conversations with your care team.', ARRAY['- Continued pelvic pressure or a lower-feeling bump','Trouble getting comfortable for long stretches','More frequent urination as pressure increases','Anticipation, worry, or both about labour']::text[], '{"focus": "Build comfort and stamina without pushing for performance.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Do 8 seated pelvic tilts and 8 shoulder rolls with slow breathing.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes, then 5 minutes of gentle squats using a chair for support (if comfortable) or standing pelvic tilts.", "feeling_good": "Swim or walk for 20 minutes, then one round of 8 supported squats, 8 wall push-ups and 8 heel raises."}, "mood_adjustment": "Anxious about birth: choose familiar, rhythmic movement over anything new. Uncomfortable: shorten and prioritise rest. Good: enjoy building stamina at a moderate pace.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Keep protein steady through the day — your own needs stay high even as stomach space shrinks, so smaller frequent portions help.', 'Frequent urination is common now from pressure, not dehydration — keep drinking normally rather than cutting back.', '{"heavy_day": "If thinking about labour feels overwhelming, you don''t have to plan anything today. One breath, one hour at a time is enough.", "a_little_low": "Name your biggest fear about birth to yourself honestly. Fear named is easier to bring to your care team than fear left vague.", "okay": "Jot down three things that matter to you about labour — pain relief, who''s in the room, movement, anything. It''s a starting list, not a contract.", "good": "Use the steadier mood to talk through your preferences with your support person or clinician.", "really_good": "Let yourself feel genuinely ready and capable today, without needing to have every detail decided."}'::jsonb, 'If you''re considering perineal massage, some clinicians suggest starting around now — ask your care team whether it''s appropriate for you and how they''d recommend doing it.', 'Write down one thing you''re looking forward to about meeting your baby.', 'Have a real conversation about what support you want during labour — not just logistics, but how you want to be treated.', 'You''re preparing thoughtfully for something no one can fully predict. That''s real courage.', 'Bring your emerging preferences to your next appointment and ask what''s realistic given your specific situation.'),
  (34, 'third', 'Practical care counts too', 'Getting ready isn''t about controlling the outcome — it''s about feeling supported either way.', 'Start the practical groundwork — hospital bag, paperwork, plans for who does what — without needing to finish it all this week.', 'Your baby''s central nervous system and lungs continue maturing. Birth at 34 weeks is still preterm and may require neonatal support. Many people begin practical preparation now; there is no single correct timetable.', ARRAY['- Increasing pelvic pressure or a feeling of "lower" positioning','More frequent Braxton Hicks','Nesting urges — or none at all','Difficulty sleeping through the night']::text[], '{"focus": "Stay comfortable and mobile while your body carries the most weight it will this pregnancy.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Sit supported and do 10 ankle circles per side and 6 slow neck rolls.", "steady": "Walk for 8 minutes, then 5 minutes of pelvic tilts and gentle side stretches.", "feeling_good": "Walk or swim for 15-20 minutes, then one round of 8 chair sit-to-stands, 8 wall push-ups and 8 heel raises."}, "mood_adjustment": "Overwhelmed by the to-do list: movement first, list second. Tired: five minutes counts. Restless with nesting energy: channel some into a gentle walk before tackling tasks.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Stock a few easy, safe snacks now for the days right after birth, when cooking will be the last thing on your mind.', 'Keep water by wherever you''re doing hospital-bag preparation — easy to forget to drink while task-focused.', '{"heavy_day": "If the practical list feels like too much, pick exactly one item and let the rest wait. Nothing needs to be finished today.", "a_little_low": "If nesting urges haven''t shown up, that''s completely normal too — it''s not a sign of anything about your readiness to parent.", "okay": "Write a loose hospital-bag list over a few sittings, not one long session. Little and often beats a perfect list all at once.", "good": "Use the steadier mood to tackle one practical task — pack a bag, sort paperwork, confirm a plan for other children or pets.", "really_good": "Enjoy the nesting energy if it''s here — a tidied corner, a washed onesie, whatever feels satisfying."}'::jsonb, 'Comfortable, easy-to-remove clothing and shoes matter more now — both for daily comfort and for whenever labour starts.', 'Pack one small, purely-for-you item in your hospital bag — music, a photo, a scent you love.', 'Divide the practical list together so it isn''t all on you — who packs what, who handles which calls.', 'However much (or little) got done, you''re building real readiness.', 'Ask which hospital or birth-centre paperwork needs to be done in advance, and when.'),
  (35, 'third', 'Understand the test before the window', 'A positive result means a plan, not a problem.', 'Understand Group B strep screening before the usual testing window, without letting it cause extra worry.', 'Depending on the country and care pathway, Group B streptococcus screening may be offered in the coming weeks. ACOG recommends 36 0/7-37 6/7 weeks. A positive result usually leads to antibiotics during labour to reduce — not eliminate — the risk of early newborn infection.', ARRAY['- Increasing pelvic pressure, sometimes described as "lower" or "dropped"','More frequent, shorter, less deep sleep','Swelling that''s more noticeable by evening','A mix of readiness and impatience']::text[], '{"focus": "Keep circulation and comfort supported as your body carries near-maximum weight.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Sit with feet elevated and do 10 toe points and 10 ankle circles per side.", "steady": "Walk for 8 minutes, then 5 minutes of calf stretches and seated pelvic tilts.", "feeling_good": "Swim or walk for 15-20 minutes, then one round of 8 supported squats and 8 heel raises."}, "mood_adjustment": "Uncomfortable: water-based movement can feel like real relief right now. Anxious about the test: choose familiar, repetitive movement. Good: keep effort easy regardless of capacity.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Keep meals easy and reliable — this is a good week to lean on whatever''s simplest rather than anything elaborate.', 'Ask your clinic if any preparation is needed before the swab test — usually none is, but confirming removes one uncertainty.', '{"heavy_day": "If test-related worry feels heavy, remember: this is a routine, common screening with a clear, effective plan either way.", "a_little_low": "Write down the one question you actually want answered about the test, and ask it directly at your appointment.", "okay": "Remind yourself that a positive result is common and well managed — it isn''t a reflection of anything you did or didn''t do.", "good": "Use the steadier mood to confirm your understanding of what happens next if the result is positive, so there''s no surprise later.", "really_good": "Let a completed test be one more thing checked off, not one more thing to worry about."}'::jsonb, 'Loose, comfortable clothing makes the swab appointment easier and more dignified.', 'Note one thing you''re proud of managing this pregnancy — practical, emotional, anything.', 'Ask someone to come to this appointment if having company would help.', 'You''re staying engaged with your care, appointment after appointment. That consistency matters.', 'Ask when and how you''ll get your GBS result, and what it means for your labour plan either way.'),
  (36, 'third', 'Nearly there, not quite yet', 'Ready and not-ready can both be true right now.', 'Hold the mix of readiness and waiting with more patience for yourself.', 'Your baby may be settling head-down, though position varies. Week 36 remains preterm until 37 weeks, and babies born now can still need additional support. Full term begins at 39 weeks.', ARRAY['- Baby feeling lower or more settled in position','More frequent, shorter-duration Braxton Hicks','Pelvic pressure that increases with standing or walking','Sleep that''s lighter and more interrupted']::text[], '{"focus": "Move in ways that feel good for your pelvis and hips as your baby settles lower.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Sit on a stability ball or firm cushion (if available) and gently rock side to side for two minutes, then rest.", "steady": "Walk for 8 minutes, then 5 minutes of hip circles and standing pelvic tilts.", "feeling_good": "Walk or swim for 15-20 minutes, then one round of 8 supported squats and 8 side-to-side weight shifts."}, "mood_adjustment": "Heavy or pressured: shorten everything and prioritise rest. Restless waiting: a walk can ease the mental waiting-game feeling. Good: enjoy moving at whatever pace suits you today.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Keep eating regularly even if appetite shrinks with less stomach room — small, frequent, nutrient-dense meals matter more than large ones now.', 'Continue your usual routine; don''t restrict fluids in hopes of easing swelling — it doesn''t help and can leave you dehydrated.', '{"heavy_day": "If the waiting feels unbearable today, that''s a real, common feeling near the end. Name it to someone rather than carrying it alone.", "a_little_low": "Say: \"This is the hardest part of waiting — the not knowing when.\" You''re allowed to find it hard.", "okay": "Notice one sign your body is preparing — however small — and let that be today''s small reassurance.", "good": "Use the steadier mood to finish one small practical task that''s been lingering.", "really_good": "Let yourself feel genuinely excited without needing to temper it \"just in case.\""}'::jsonb, 'If your clinician has approved perineal massage, this is a common window to begin, done gently, a few times a week.', 'Write a short note to your baby, whenever they arrive.', 'Talk through what you''ll each do the moment labour starts — who calls whom, who does what first.', 'You''ve carried this pregnancy almost the whole way. Nearly there is still an achievement.', 'Ask about your baby''s current position and what it might mean for labour, if known.'),
  (37, 'third', 'Early term, real patience', 'Your due date is an estimate, not a deadline.', 'Practise patience with the waiting while staying alert to real labour signs.', 'You have reached early term (37 0/7-38 6/7 weeks), not yet full term. Many pregnancies continue for several more weeks, and each additional day supports ongoing maturation when there is no medical reason for earlier birth.', ARRAY['- Increasing pelvic pressure or a feeling your baby has "dropped"','Loss of some mucus plug — or no noticeable change','More frequent Braxton Hicks that may feel more real','A strong urge to have things ready']::text[], '{"focus": "Stay gently active — walking remains one of the most comfortable options for most people now.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Sit supported and take five minutes of slow, belly-focused breathing.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes at a comfortable pace, then 5 minutes of hip circles and pelvic tilts.", "feeling_good": "Walk or swim for 20 minutes, keeping the pace whatever feels good — there''s no benefit to pushing harder now."}, "mood_adjustment": "Achy or pressured: shorten and rest more. Anxious about timing: choose a familiar route close to home. Good: enjoy it, your body knows what it''s doing.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, contractions that become regular and don''t ease with rest, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Keep meals light and easy to digest if appetite has shrunk — frequent small portions are completely sufficient.', 'Staying well hydrated may help reduce excess Braxton Hicks — keep your usual routine going.', '{"heavy_day": "If every twinge feels like \"is this it,\" that''s exhausting and completely normal. Rest when you can, and trust that real labour will make itself clear.", "a_little_low": "Say: \"My due date is a guess, not a promise.\" Most babies don''t arrive exactly on it, and that''s not a delay, it''s biology.", "okay": "Review the real difference again: true labour contractions get closer together, stronger, and don''t ease with rest. Braxton Hicks usually do ease.", "good": "Use the steadier mood to confirm your hospital bag, contacts and transport plan are genuinely ready.", "really_good": "Let the anticipation feel exciting today rather than tense — you''re genuinely close now."}'::jsonb, 'Rest whenever your body asks for it — there''s no value in "saving up energy" a particular way; sleep when you can.', 'Write down what you''re most looking forward to about meeting your baby.', 'Do a final walk-through together: bag, route to the hospital or birth centre, who to call first.', 'You''ve reached early term. However this arrives, you''ve done the hardest waiting already.', 'Ask directly: at this stage, what should make you call — and what can wait until your next appointment?'),
  (38, 'third', 'Almost, and still not rushing', 'You do not have to force labour to begin. Stay comfortable and follow your care plan.', 'Stay grounded through the uncertainty of not knowing exactly when.', 'Your baby continues maturing and practising newborn reflexes. The exact day labour begins cannot be predicted. Avoid unproven attempts to start labour and ask your clinician before trying any induction method.', ARRAY['- Strong pelvic pressure, sometimes described as "lightning crotch"','Continued or increasing Braxton Hicks','Nesting energy, exhaustion, or both','Difficulty imagining anything beyond the birth itself']::text[], '{"focus": "Keep movement gentle, familiar and comfortable — nothing here needs to \"bring on\" labour.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Sit or lie on your side and breathe slowly for five minutes, allowing your body to settle without using exercise to test movement.", "steady": "Walk for 10 minutes at whatever pace feels good, then 5 minutes of hip circles.", "feeling_good": "Walk or swim for 20 minutes at a comfortable pace — movement now is about comfort, not about speeding anything up."}, "mood_adjustment": "Uncomfortable: rest is just as valid as movement this week. Restless: a slow walk can ease mental tension even if it changes nothing physically. Good: enjoy it without any pressure to \"do more.\"", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, contractions that become regular and don''t ease with rest, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Keep something easy and appealing on hand — appetite can be unpredictable this close to the end.', 'Continue your usual fluid routine; there''s no special adjustment needed this week.', '{"heavy_day": "If not knowing feels unbearable today, that''s real. Say it out loud to someone rather than carrying it silently.", "a_little_low": "Notice if you''re trying to \"make\" labour happen through sheer willpower. You don''t have to — your body doesn''t need your permission.", "okay": "Give yourself one grounding ritual for today — a walk, a bath, a call with someone who makes you feel calm.", "good": "Use the steadier mood to enjoy something completely unrelated to pregnancy for a while.", "really_good": "Let today be genuinely enjoyable. There''s no rule that the final stretch has to be tense."}'::jsonb, 'A warm shower may ease general discomfort. If you suspect your waters have broken, note the time and fluid colour and contact your maternity team promptly for instructions.', 'Write a short letter to yourself for the hardest moment of labour, to read back to yourself when it counts.', 'Spend deliberate time together not talking about labour — a meal, a film, anything ordinary.', 'You are, quite literally, almost there.', 'Ask what happens at your appointments from here, and when they''d start discussing next steps if labour hasn''t begun.'),
  (39, 'third', 'Any day, and today is still today', 'However this begins, you have already done so much to get here.', 'Stay present in today, rather than living entirely in "not yet."', 'Your pregnancy is considered full term from 39 0/7 weeks. Your baby continues maturing while you and your care team watch for labour or any reason the plan should change.', ARRAY['- Strong, frequent Braxton Hicks','Increased pelvic pressure or a change in vaginal discharge','Waves of readiness, impatience, fear or calm — sometimes all in one day','Trouble sleeping, whether from discomfort or anticipation']::text[], '{"focus": "Whatever feels comfortable today is the right amount.", "tiers": {"heavy": "Sit or lie comfortably and breathe slowly, hands on your bump, for five minutes.", "steady": "A slow, familiar walk for 10-15 minutes, whatever pace feels right today.", "feeling_good": "Walk or swim at a comfortable pace for as long as feels good, stopping well before you''re tired."}, "mood_adjustment": "However you feel today is the right starting point — there''s no version of this week that needs a specific plan followed.", "safety": "Use stable support, breathe continuously and keep effort conversational. Stop for pain, bleeding, dizziness, chest pain, breathlessness before exertion, fluid leakage, contractions that become regular and don''t ease with rest, calf pain/swelling, or a change in your baby''s usual movement."}'::jsonb, 'Eat what appeals to you and sits well — light, familiar foods are completely fine if that''s what feels right.', 'Keep your usual routine going — steady, regular sips through the day.', '{"heavy_day": "If today feels like too much waiting, it''s okay to just get through it. You don''t need to feel ready — you just need to be here.", "a_little_low": "Whatever mix of feelings you''re holding right now — fear, impatience, grief for pregnancy ending, excitement — all of it is allowed at once.", "okay": "Notice what is true right now: you are here, you have a care plan, and you know who to contact if something changes.", "good": "Use the steadier mood to do something that has nothing to do with being \"ready\" — rest, a favourite meal, quiet company.", "really_good": "Let yourself feel proud today, regardless of what tomorrow brings."}'::jsonb, 'Keep your routine as normal and comforting as possible — familiar rhythms can genuinely help these last uncertain days feel calmer.', 'Write one line about how you feel right at this moment, to look back on later.', 'Reconfirm the plan together one more time — and then let it rest. You''ve prepared enough.', 'You have reached full term. However your baby arrives, this is already a real accomplishment.', 'Confirm exactly what your clinic wants you to do once labour starts, and keep their number somewhere easy to find.')
on conflict (week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  priority = excluded.priority,
  journey = excluded.journey,
  what_you_may_notice = excluded.what_you_may_notice,
  move = excluded.move,
  nourish = excluded.nourish,
  hydration_goal = excluded.hydration_goal,
  reset = excluded.reset,
  care_for_yourself = excluded.care_for_yourself,
  your_corner = excluded.your_corner,
  support_moment = excluded.support_moment,
  celebrate_this_week = excluded.celebrate_this_week,
  for_your_care_team = excluded.for_your_care_team;